# Commits that changed formatting only, which `git blame` should look past.
#
# Without this, one mechanical pass owns the authorship of every line it
# touched, and finding out why a line of geometry says what it says means
# walking back through a commit that only moved whitespace.
#
# GitHub reads this file automatically in its blame view. The command line does
# not, so each clone enables it once:
#
#     git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
#
# Add a rev here only when the commit provably changed no content. This is a way
# to keep authorship readable, not a way to hide a change that mattered -- a
# commit listed here is one nobody will be shown again.
#
# One full 40-character SHA per line. An abbreviated SHA is rejected, and so is
# a rev that no longer exists, with a fatal error rather than a warning -- so a
# rewritten history means revisiting this file.

